# The Altium-style interaction profile's hotkeys, in the format of a user.hotkeys file: action name, key, alternate
# key, separated by tabs. A line that starts with # is a comment. See docs/reckless/interaction-profile.md, 5.2, 5.3
# and 6. Names are registered action names.
#
# A key bound here is taken: an action not named here loses it as a compiled default. It is taken in the scope of the
# action that binds it: a common.* action is in every editor, so its key is taken in every editor, and an eeschema.* or
# pcbnew.* action takes its key in that editor only. A key that means one thing in the schematic editor and another in
# the PCB editor is therefore bound by two editor-prefixed actions.
#
# What is left out, and why:
#   - Ctrl+PgDn: the schematic fits all objects, the PCB editor fits the document, and both are common.* actions, which
#     one table cannot bind to the same key for two editors. Both are reachable as sequences (V F, V D).
#   - Ctrl+Shift+G: the schematic toggles the visible grid and the PCB editor the grid overrides, the same clash. The
#     PCB editor's is its stock key; the schematic's is the sequence V G V.
#   - Right-click and Esc in a command: right-click is the input.right_click_cancels setting, Esc is KiCad's own.
#   - The in-command keys (Space, Shift+Space, L, X, Y and Tab) are bound below to context aliases, which act only while
#     the move or placement loop they belong to runs; with no command running, those keys mean what they mean idle.
#   - Routing keys, in-command and sticky: step 5.
#   - Anything that needs a new action and is not built yet: the heads-up display, nudging, in-place editing, the 3D
#     keys.
#
# Roots of the key sequences: the letter is typed first, then the letters of the entries it leads to (6.1 and 6.3).
common.Sequence.place	P
common.Sequence.edit	E
common.Sequence.move	M
common.Sequence.select	S
common.Sequence.deselect	X
common.Sequence.align	A
common.Sequence.jump	J
common.Sequence.view	V
common.Sequence.project	C
common.Sequence.design	D
common.Sequence.tools	T
common.Sequence.reports	R
common.Sequence.options	O
common.Sequence.file	F
eeschema.Sequence.zoom	Z
pcbnew.Sequence.route	U
pcbnew.Sequence.nets	N
#
# Direct keys of both editors (6.2 and 6.4)
common.Control.toggleUnits	Q
common.Control.zoomCenter	Home
common.Control.zoomRedraw	End
common.Control.zoomIn	PgUp
common.Control.zoomOut	PgDn
# G and Shift+G cycle the grid, in the schematic editor; the PCB editors take G for the grid list below (step 6) and keep
# Shift+G on the previous grid until the heads-up display (step 8) takes it
common.Control.gridNext	G
common.Control.gridPrev	Shift+G
common.Control.editGrids	Ctrl+G
common.Control.toggleGridOverrides	Ctrl+Shift+G
common.Control.cursorUpFast	Shift+Up
common.Control.cursorDownFast	Shift+Down
common.Control.cursorLeftFast	Shift+Left
common.Control.cursorRightFast	Shift+Right
common.Interactive.duplicate	Ctrl+R
common.Interactive.measureTool	Ctrl+M
common.Interactive.selectAll	Ctrl+A
common.Control.showProperties	F11
common.Interactive.search	Shift+F12
common.Control.highContrastModeCycle	Shift+S
common.Control.magneticSnapToggle	Shift+E
# The key list: the context menu of the running command at the cursor, with the keys of its commands (5.5)
common.Control.showToolContextMenu	Shift+F1	`
#
# Schematic editor (6.2)
eeschema.InteractiveDrawingLineWireBus.drawWires	Ctrl+W
eeschema.InteractiveDrawingLineWireBus.switchPosture	Space
eeschema.EditorControl.clearHighlight	Shift+C
# In-command keys of the placement and move loops: context aliases that forward to the real action (5.5). Where a
# key is also the root of a sequence (X), the command that is running wins.
eeschema.InteractiveMove.rotateCCWWhileMoving	Space
eeschema.InteractiveMove.rotateCWWhileMoving	Shift+Space
eeschema.InteractiveMove.mirrorHWhileMoving	X
eeschema.InteractiveMove.mirrorVWhileMoving	Y
eeschema.InteractiveMove.propertiesWhileMoving	Tab
eeschema.InteractiveDrawing.rotateCCWWhilePlacing	Space
eeschema.InteractiveDrawing.rotateCWWhilePlacing	Shift+Space
eeschema.InteractiveDrawing.mirrorHWhilePlacing	X
eeschema.InteractiveDrawing.mirrorVWhilePlacing	Y
eeschema.InteractiveDrawing.propertiesWhilePlacing	Tab
# Tab held "next net item" in KiCad; here it is properties, and the command is in Edit > Select (E S N)
eeschema.EditorControl.nextNetItem	
#
# PCB editor (6.4)
pcbnew.EditorControl.clearHighlight	Shift+C
pcbnew.Control.flipBoard	Ctrl+F
pcbnew.Control.netColorMode	F5
pcbnew.Control.layerNext	Num Pad *
pcbnew.Control.layerPrev	Shift+Num Pad *
# Views (5.7). L and Ctrl+D show the Appearance panel with the focus on its Layers or Objects tab; L typed in the panel
# gives the focus back to the canvas. L is also the in-command flip key below, which wins while a move or a placement
# runs. [ and ] step the mask level (the high-contrast dimming factor), also the net highlight's. G opens the grid list
# at the cursor; the common.Control.gridNext above, which also holds G, loses it in the PCB editors (a pcbnew. action
# takes its key there). Ctrl+D was Duplicate, which is Ctrl+R here.
pcbnew.Control.showAppearanceLayers	L
pcbnew.Control.showAppearanceObjects	Ctrl+D
pcbnew.Control.maskLevelDown	[
pcbnew.Control.maskLevelUp	]
pcbnew.Control.showGridMenu	G
# In-command keys of the move and placement loops (5.5), and the router's width and via editor
pcbnew.InteractiveEdit.rotateCcwWhileMoving	Space
pcbnew.InteractiveEdit.rotateCwWhileMoving	Shift+Space
pcbnew.InteractiveEdit.flipWhileMoving	L
pcbnew.InteractiveEdit.propertiesWhileMoving	Tab
pcbnew.InteractiveDrawing.rotateCcwWhilePlacing	Space
pcbnew.InteractiveDrawing.rotateCwWhilePlacing	Shift+Space
pcbnew.InteractiveDrawing.flipWhilePlacing	L
pcbnew.InteractiveDrawing.propertiesWhilePlacing	Tab
pcbnew.InteractiveRouter.CustomTrackViaSize	Tab
# Tab held "skip" while moving footprints one by one; it is in that command's right-click menu
pcbnew.InteractiveEdit.skip	
pcbnew.InteractiveRouter.OptimizeSelected	Ctrl+Alt+G
pcbnew.InteractiveRouter.SingleTrack	Ctrl+W
# Ctrl+W was the router's corner-style switch, which step 5 rebinds; no key until then, so it cannot fight the above
pcbnew.InteractiveRouter.SwitchRounding45	
